 youngest  candidate but I'm the longest party  member and I've been the longest  activist for the libertarian cause  specifically so I start out as a street  activist in 2007 I would walk from  subway car to subway car with Ron Paul's  book foreign policy of freedom and I  would rien Paul speeches to the subway  citizens and just interrupt here did you  like wake up one morning and read this  book and it transformed your life or  look how did you get involved  I was inspired when I saw his debate  with Rudy Giuliani and I was paying New  York City income taxes and I was upset  so I wanted a candidate that I could  support and when I saw Ron Paul sort of  smacked down Jolie Giuliani on foreign  policy it was sort of an awakening  moment and I went downtown Manhattan and  myself and twelve other gentlemen turned  that an old nightclub into a 1200  persons strong organization we raise a  million dollars for Ron Paul and then I  got a job with the libertarian party  thanks to that activism I'm very good at  organizing I'm kind of the community  organizer for libertarians and I build  online communities mostly digital  communities of people who are of like  mind and what I learned was that the  libertarian movement when unified and  purpose is very powerful so while I was  working at the libertarian party I put  together a memo with the internship  staff that I had created how to get  libertarians on TV Judge Napolitano who  is another person who I was a great fan  of who had a little podcast on Fox it  was just a little digital thing and so I  stood up one day with David Bose at a  meeting and I said David let's get the  judges on show and that was successful  so I launched a show on Fox Business  from outside of Fox and I became the  judges producer he called me the right  side of his brain and I learned  everything that I know about the  Constitution from the judge and kind of  fought the trenches beside him as his  a-one producer and I think that I'm the  best person to carry these ideas forward  because I can articulate them to the  American people in a way that isn't  quite as off-putting perhaps as some of  our approaches in the past and I'm not  seen as sort of like just a pie in the  sky hi governor you know from New Mexico  where people look at him and say is he  here is he is he awake is he asleep  and and they see me as energetic and  exciting and ready to lead a movement  that I had started on the streets of New  York City in back from Kansas City into  Washington DC your pitch has been  largely to Ted Cruz voters it's been to  Rand Paul voters it's been Mary Madeline  gave you an endorsement people who are  not actually necessarily traditionally  Republicans are you sort of in that cat  if we were if John McAfee is Donald  Trump in the libertarian race Gary  Johnson is I don't know Romney who is  Austin Peterson like what what's your  well  it's a combination of Bernie Sanders and  Barack Obama in a sense you know I was  not really seeing the Bernie Sanders to  be honest with well I'll tell you why  because it's my donor base you know I'm  the grassroots candidate I'm the one  that thousands and thousands of people  are sending their their money in their  change jar to I started this campaign  mailing bumper stickers and licking  stamps by myself and now I have  marshaled the full might of the  grassroots of the libertarian movement  because I know how powerful they can be  and when they have someone that they can  believe in that they know represents  them they're gonna go out in the streets  and they're gonna do the same thing that  I did ten years ago and I know how  powerful that can be in recruitment so  that's what I'm trying to do what would  failure look like for the Libertarian  Party in November what is a numeric  level a psychic level if they don't get  beyond that they will have missed an  opportunity I think probably the same as  they got last time that's why I'm  running is because I was nervous that  t